"Internal Error ()\nciteturn101view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://atomdrift.org/feed.xml\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://atomdrift.org/feed.xml: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncleave — Software Decomposition Engine - The Atomdrift Project (https://atomdrift.org/cleave/)\nciteturn101view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://atomdrift.org/cleave/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 74\nL0: 【0†atomdrift Open-source, local-first supply-chain attack detection】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†GitHub†github.com】 【2†Install ↓†install.atomdrift.org】 【3†Lab†lab.atomdrift.org】\nL3: \nL4: 【0†Home】 【4†News】 【5†Mission】 【6†Compare】 【7†Projects】 【8†Support】\nL5: \nL6: 【9†Image: cleave】 beta\nL7: \nL8: 【10†Overview】 【11†Getting started】 【12†How it works】 【13†Integrations】 【14†FAQ】\nL9: cleave answers one question: what can this program do? It unpacks the file, extracts structural facts, and matches them against the public 【15†Atomdrift traits†github.com】 rule set, aligned broadly to 【16†MBC†github.com】 and 【17†MITRE ATT&CK†attack.mitre.org】. Every finding points back to the strings, symbols, imports, or metadata that produced it. Apache-2.0, no telemetry.\nL10:   * Triage. Run it on a release, a suspicious sample, or a directory of dropped files. `cleave diff old/ new/` shows new capabilities, tampered headers, and provenance anomalies between versions.\nL11:   * Features for other tools. Stable JSON schema, deterministic output, SHA-256-keyed cache, and a Rust library. 【18†Atomdrift Scan】 is the reference downstream classifier.\nL12: \nL13: 【19†Image: cleave analyze — capabilities of a single sample】\nL14: \nL15: 【20†Image: cleave diff — what changed between two releases】\nL16: ## What cleave analyzes\nL17:   * Binaries and bytecode — Mach-O, ELF, PE, WebAssembly, Android DEX, BEAM, MSI, CHM, PyInstaller, Java `.class`, Python `.pyc`, static libraries\nL18:   * Source (24+ languages, tree-sitter) — Python, JS/TS, Go, Rust, C/C++, Java, Kotlin, C#, Swift, ObjC, Ruby, PHP, Perl, Lua, Shell, PowerShell, Groovy, Scala, Zig, Elixir, Clojure, Batch, VBScript\nL19:   * Archives (recursive) — zip, tar (gz/bz2/xz/zst), 7z, rar, cab, jar/war, deb, rpm, pkg, apk, gem, crate, whl, nupkg, phar, vsix, xpi, crx, ipa, epub\nL20:   * Documents & data — PDF structure, RTF, LNK shortcut metadata, Office (OLE2 + OOXML), OpenDocument, plist, HTML, XML, Markdown, PNG/JPEG, package manifests, GitHub Actions, systemd units, XDG `.desktop`\nL21: ## Open, inspectable rules\nL22: \nL23: The rule set is a mix. Hand-written rules are precise for the patterns humans understand well. AI-derived rules come from a training corpus of about a million samples and cover the long tail across 20+ languages, which no team keeps up with by hand.\nL24: The AI runs at build time, not at runtime. Given the same bytes, traits bundle, options, and analysis tools, cleave's findings are deterministic. No runtime model, GPU, or upload. Reports carry a timestamp, so the serialized bytes differ between runs even when the findings do not.\nL25: \nL26: The first run downloads the compatible traits bundle. cleave also checks for a new release at most once a day; `CLEAVE_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1` stops it.\nL27: ## Install\nL28: \nL29:     brew install atomdrift-project/tap/cleave             # macOS / Linux\nL30: \nL31: The latest release, 3.0.0-beta.1, is source-only. Homebrew currently installs 2.12.1. Prebuilt binaries from earlier releases for macOS (signed and notarized), Linux, Windows, FreeBSD, OpenBSD, NetBSD, Solaris, and illumos are on the 【21†releases page†github.com】.\nL32: \nL33: From source (Rust 1.97+):\nL34: \nL35:     git clone https://github.com/atomdrift-project/cleave.git\nL36:     cd cleave\nL37:     make install\nL38: ## Usage\nL39: \nL40:     cleave suspect.bin                            # single sample\nL41:     cleave /tmp/box-o-malware                     # recursive, unpacks archives\nL42:     cleave diff v1.2.0/ v1.3.0/                   # release-to-release diff\nL43:     cleave --format jsonl --min-crit suspicious   # streaming JSON for pipelines\nL44: \nL45: Optional: 【22†rizin†github.com】 for disassembly, 【23†upx†github.com】 for unpacking, and upstream 【24†7-Zip†7-zip.org】 for disk images (p7zip cannot read APFS, so `.dmg` contents go unscanned).\nL46: ### Links\nL47: \nL48:   * 【25†Source code†github.com】\nL49:   * 【21†Releases†github.com】\nL50:   * 【26†Bug tracker†github.com】\nL51: \nL52: ### Contributing\nL53: \nL54:   * 【25†Fork on GitHub†github.com】\nL55:   * 【26†Open an issue†github.com】\nL56: \nL57: ### Project Info\nL58: \nL59: Version\nL60:     3.0.0-beta.1\nL61: Release Date\nL62:     2026-10-13\nL63: License\nL64:     Apache 2.0\nL65: Language\nL66:     Rust\nL67: \nL68: ### Related\nL69: \nL70:   * 【27†stng】 — string extraction\nL71:   * 【18†scan】 — AI malware detection\nL72: \nL73: © 2026 The Atomdrift Project\n"
